<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.20.xsd">

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         067-match-history-visibility

         One column: whether a player publishes their match history.

         The profile's Matches tab was a dead end for everyone except the player
         themselves — on a *public* profile, that is every visitor. This makes it
         the player's own decision rather than a system rule.

         WHY NO RLS POLICY HERE.

         The obvious shape would be a policy widening matches_select_participant
         (008-security, 0016) to admit any match with an opted-in participant,
         the way matches_select_event (017, 0028) widened it by event. That is
         deliberately NOT done, because of how this feature has to behave.

         A doubles match carries four names. If one player publishes their record
         and the other three did not, the three must still not be named — their
         participation is their own fact. So the rule is not row-level at all: the
         match ROW is publishable by one participant, while each participant's
         NAME is gated by that participant. RLS is row-level and cannot express
         per-row column masking; column grants are not per-row either.

         So the gate lives in the API layer, in the one endpoint that serves this
         (GET /api/v1/players/{playerId}/matches), which reads through the
         service role and redacts each unopted participant before responding —
         the same pattern, and the same reason, as the match domain's other
         service-role paths ("checks a self-service RLS policy can't express
         cleanly", 008-security, changeSet 0017's note).

         The consequence worth stating plainly: because RLS is unchanged, a
         direct PostgREST query still returns only matches the caller played in.
         That endpoint is the sole path to another player's history, which is
         what makes the redaction real rather than cosmetic.

         DEFAULT false: this preserves today's behaviour exactly. No existing
         player's history becomes visible because this column arrived; every
         profile that publishes has opted in.
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <changeSet id="0001-player-profiles-show-match-history" author="claude">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <columnExists tableName="player_profiles" columnName="show_match_history"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>
        <addColumn tableName="player_profiles">
            <column name="show_match_history"
                    type="boolean"
                    defaultValueBoolean="false"
                    remarks="Whether this player's match history is readable on their public profile. Enforced in GET /api/v1/players/{playerId}/matches, not in RLS — see this changelog's header for why.">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </addColumn>
        <rollback>
            <dropColumn tableName="player_profiles" columnName="show_match_history"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
